    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Dilation Given a Vector and its Image",
    "problem": "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}72 \\\\ -40\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$",
    "worked_solution": "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}72 \\\\ -40\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=8v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=8.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
